Ammunition comes in four families, each in a long and a short version.  A bow
accepts exactly one of the eight and refuses everything else, so what you can
carry is decided by what you can shoot.

     Long Stalk    : Hunting Arrows
     Short Stalk   : Fighting Arrows
     Long Quarrel  : Squabble Quarrels
     Short Quarrel : Common Quarrels
     Long Dart     : Sniper Blowdarts
     Short Dart    : Common Blowdarts
     Long Pellet   : Heavy Pellets
     Short Pellet  : Common Pellets

EVALUATE will tell you which family a piece of ammunition belongs to, and will
tell you the same of a bow, so the two can be matched before you buy.

Each family behaves differently once it reaches its target.

STALKS are ordinary arrows.  Short stalks are quick to nock, and a practised
archer can loose several in the time it takes to draw a longer shaft - see
FAST LOAD.  Long stalks cannot be hurried.  They are drawn by main strength,
and a powerful bow needs a powerful archer even to bend it; the stronger you
are and the greater the bow, the harder the shaft strikes.  A long stalk is
the heaviest single shot available to an archer, paid for in rate of fire.

QUARRELS are driven by the bow's mechanism rather than by your arm, so they
neither reward strength nor permit haste.  What they do is bite.  A quarrel
lodges in a wound far more readily than a loosed arrow.

DARTS are the assassin's ammunition.  They are silent, they take poison
readily, and in a thief's hands a quiet, unseen approach counts for as much as
marksmanship - much as it does for a backstab.  Other classes can shoot them,
but only a thief gets anything for the stalking.

PELLETS have no point at all.  They batter rather than pierce, never lodge in
a wound, and leave deep bruising instead.  A hard pellet against an unarmoured
target is punishing; a soft one against armour is wasted effort.

Ammunition wears out.  Every hit dulls a shaft a little, and a dulled shaft is
far less likely to lodge in its target - which matters, because an embedded
missile goes on causing harm long after it struck, and can turn a clean wound
septic.  A dull arrow can be brought back with a whetstone; see SHARPEN.  Wear
does not stop at the edge, and ammunition that has been used hard enough will
eventually come apart.  What the head is made of decides how long it lasts: a
steel head shot into unarmoured flesh will outlive its owner, while a wooden
one splinters against anything solid.

